cards.back:
Echo only returns a value already typed as `T`. Measure fails because unconstrained `T` does not guarantee a `Length` member. Declare the capability and constrain `T`:

```csharp
interface IHasLength
{
    int Length { get; }
}

static int Measure<T>(T value) where T : IHasLength
    => value.Length;
```

Only types implementing `IHasLength` satisfy this contract; a matching property alone is insufficient.

```csharp
static T Inspect<T>(T value) where T : IHasLength
{
    _ = value.Length;
    return value;
}

var result = Inspect(new Label());
```

`T` is inferred as `Label`, so the return type is `Label`. The interface guarantees the only required member. `class` and `struct` would restrict the type category without helping the operation; `new()` is unnecessary because the method creates no `T`. A type argument that does not implement the interface is rejected at compile time.
